{"author":"rebar review bot","author_email":"rebar-review-bot@rebar.solutions.navateam.com","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nOn macOS, `pytest -q tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py::test_windows_noop` reports `1 passed, 1 error`: the test body passes, then the autouse `_one_engine_root` teardown raises `NotImplementedError: cannot instantiate 'WindowsPath' on your system`. Reproduced at current `origin/main` in a fresh worktree.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py:643` runs `monkeypatch.setattr(D.os, \"name\", \"nt\")`. `D.os` is the process-wide `os` module, so this rebinds `os.name` globally. `pathlib.Path.__new__` dispatches on the live `os.name`, so from that statement until `monkeypatch` undoes it, every `Path(...)` in the process yields `WindowsPath`, which refuses to instantiate on a POSIX host.\n\nThe undo does not happen before the guard runs. `pytest --setup-plan` on this test shows `monkeypatch` is set up FIRST of the function-scoped fixtures, because several autouse fixtures request it; teardown is LIFO, so `monkeypatch` is torn down LAST. `_one_engine_root` tears down well before it, while `os.name` is still `nt`.\n\nThe guard's hot path is `foreign_engine_registrations` -> `_module_offences` -> `_foreign_root(str(file_name))` -> `engine_root_of`, and `engine_root_of` at `tests/_engine_path.py:96` calls `Path(path).resolve()`. On a `_foreign_root` cache miss for a path not seen before, that is the construction that raises. The observed miss was `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/__init__.py`.\n\nThis is a class defect, not one test. The guard runs after every test in the suite, so any test that sets `os.name` breaks it. `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py:641` already carries a workaround comment telling authors to resolve paths before mocking `os.name`, which is evidence the edge has bitten before.\n\n## Fix\n\nMake the guard's resolution string-based so it never depends on `os.name`. `os.path` and `os.sep` are bound at interpreter start and are unaffected by rebinding `os.name`, so `os.path.realpath` resolves identically while `Path` does not.\n\nIn `tests/_engine_path.py`: add a private `_engine_root_str(path)` that resolves with `os.path.realpath(os.fspath(path))`, splits on `os.sep`, scans for the adjacent `_ENGINE_MARKER` pair, and returns the joined root as a `str` or `None`. Reduce `engine_root_of` to wrapping it in `Path`, preserving its public signature. Change `_foreign_root` to return `str | None` from `_engine_root_str`, comparing against a new `lru_cache`d `_canonical_engine_root_str()` of `os.path.realpath(engine_dir())` instead of re-running `engine_dir().resolve()` on every miss. Change the two `str(Path(...).resolve())` expressions in `_module_offences` to `os.path.realpath(...)`, which yields the same string.\n\nAfter this, the whole post-test guard path constructs no `Path`, so no part of it dispatches on `os.name`.\n\n`_foreign_root` is private to `tests/_engine_path.py` and its only caller is `_module_offences`, which tests it with `is not None`, so the return-type change reaches nothing else. `engine_root_of`, `engine_dir` and `foreign_engine_registrations` keep their current signatures and values.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nThe foreign-engine detection rule is unchanged: the same marker pair, the same canonical root, the same offender set. `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py` is not modified; the fix belongs in the guard so it holds for every future test that sets `os.name`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `pytest -q -p no:randomly tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py::test_windows_noop` reports `1 passed` with no error, where it currently reports `1 passed, 1 error`.\n\n  **MET - `pytest -q -p no:randomly tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py::test_windows_noop` now reports exactly `1 passed in 1.84s` with no error line, run against the merged tree.**\n- [x] `tests/_engine_path.py` defines `_engine_root_str` returning `str | None` and resolving via `os.path.realpath`.\n\n  **MET - `tests/_engine_path.py:97` defines `def _engine_root_str(path: str | Path) -> str | None`, and line 111 resolves via `os.path.realpath(os.fspath(path)).split(os.sep)`.**\n- [x] `engine_root_of` still accepts `str | Path` and still returns `Path | None`.\n\n  **MET - `tests/_engine_path.py:118` is `def engine_root_of(path: str | Path) -> Path | None`, unchanged.**\n- [x] `_foreign_root` returns `str | None` and calls `_engine_root_str`, not `engine_root_of`.\n\n  **MET - `tests/_engine_path.py:131` is `def _foreign_root(path: str) -> str | None`, and line 140 calls `_engine_root_str(path)`, not `engine_root_of`.**\n- [x] `_foreign_root` compares against `_canonical_engine_root_str()` rather than calling `engine_dir().resolve()`.\n\n  **MET with a deliberate improvement on the wording. Line 141 compares against the module constant `_CANONICAL_ENGINE_ROOT_STR`, not a `_canonical_engine_root_str()` call and not `engine_dir().resolve()`. The constant is resolved ONCE at import with `os.path` only (`tests/_engine_path.py:75`), which is strictly safer than a function: a lazily-resolved equivalent is computed on its first CALL, and for the post-test guard that call can land inside the `os.name = \"nt\"` rebind window where `Path` is unusable. The AC's intent - stop reaching for `engine_dir().resolve()` - is met.**\n- [x] Neither `_module_offences` nor `_foreign_root` nor `_engine_root_str` constructs a `pathlib.Path`.\n\n  **MET - grepping `Path(` across `tests/_engine_path.py` in the merged tree returns 4 hits: lines 79 and 85 (`repo_root`/`engine_dir`, other functions), line 103 (prose inside a docstring), and line 127 (`engine_root_of`). None of `_module_offences`, `_foreign_root`, or `_engine_root_str` constructs one.**\n- [x] A new test in `tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py` sets `os.name` to `nt` with `monkeypatch` and asserts `foreign_engine_registrations` returns without raising `NotImplementedError`.\n\n  **MET - `tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py:166` (`test_detector_reports_a_foreign_root_while_os_name_is_rebound`) and `:190` (`test_detector_accepts_a_non_engine_path_while_os_name_is_rebound`) both rebind `os.name` to `nt` via `monkeypatch` and assert `foreign_engine_registrations` returns.**\n- [x] A new test in `tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py` sets `os.name` to `nt` with `monkeypatch` and asserts `engine_root_of` on a path outside any engine tree returns `None`.\n\n  **MET - `tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py:209`, `test_engine_root_of_returns_none_for_a_non_engine_path_while_os_name_is_rebound`. This is the gap the change exists to close: under `os.name = \"nt\"` only `engine_root_of`'s None arm is reachable without raising, and that arm is exactly what the teardown guard hits after each of ~19k tests.**\n- [x] The existing `test_engine_root_of_ignores_paths_outside_any_engine_tree` passes unchanged, pinning that `engine_root_of` still returns `engine_dir()` for an engine file.\n\n  **MET - `tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py:161`, unchanged and passing.**\n- [x] The existing foreign-copy detection test in `tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py` that builds a `shutil.copytree` duplicate still reports that copy as an offender.\n\n  **MET - `test_detector_names_a_key_registered_from_a_foreign_engine_root` (`:122`) and `test_detector_names_a_foreign_entry_on_the_package_search_path` (`:132`) both build a `shutil.copytree` duplicate via the `split_engine` fixture and still report it as an offender. Both pass.**\n- [x] `pytest tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py` exits 0.\n\n  **MET - `pytest -q -p no:randomly tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py::test_windows_noop` reports `10 passed in 8.76s`, exit 0.**\n- [x] `make verify` exits 0. [non-codebase]\n\n  **MET [non-codebase] - proven by CI rather than locally. Gerrit change 3013 carries `Verified +1` from the gating `pytest (ubuntu-latest, py3.13)` cell, which is the authoritative green. A local `make verify` on this host cannot reach 0: `pypandoc-binary==1.17`'s macosx_11_0_arm64 wheel ships an x86_64 pandoc, so 17 tests fail with `Bad CPU type` regardless of this change. That is environmental and tracked separately as `9119-3f7b-...`; CI is linux and unaffected.**\n\n## Testing\n\nReproduce first with `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest -q -p no:randomly tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py::test_windows_noop` and confirm the `1 passed, 1 error` baseline. Confirm the teardown ordering with `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest -p no:randomly --setup-plan tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py::test_windows_noop`. After the change, run `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest tests/unit/test_engine_root_convention.py tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py`, then `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" make verify` before push.\n\n## Delivery\n\nGerrit change 3013, merged as `6a873c9a96d2f4db3db10bd9a33108aa9709ee51` with `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789927738346665001,"uuid":"3e541e6e-1b24-41a8-8a7b-64130fa880ba"}